    {
      "id": "MJUe",
      "code_hash": "811f23f2509e00ec897f753682d01127",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"multiclass-classifiers\">Multiclass classifiers</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">This notebook shows how EBaMl reads an EBM with three or more classes: the kind of the model, the graph with one\nseries per class, the options that select and shift the classes, and the description by the LLM.</span>\n<ul>\n<li>You need no data download: the iris data comes with scikit-learn.</li>\n<li>Everything outside the buttons runs without a key and sends no request.</li>\n<li>The buttons at the end send real requests. The default model needs <code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code>. Pass <code>model=</code> to use\n  another model, as the documentation page \"Choosing the model\" shows.</li>\n</ul></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "vblA",
      "code_hash": "e69f07d2dc06a1d336adfff55b89057f",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"a-multiclass-ebm\">A multiclass EBM</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The iris data has three classes of flower and four features. <code>model_kind</code> reads the kind of the fitted EBM.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "bkHC",
      "code_hash": "0c780af377678988121395a7809e2dca",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\"><code>model_kind(ebm)</code> is <code>MulticlassClassifier(classes=('setosa', 'versicolor', 'virginica'))</code>, with the outputs <code>['setosa', 'versicolor', 'virginica']</code>.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Each bin has one score per class. The difference between the scores of two classes is the change of the log-odds of one class against the other, so only the differences between the classes change the predicted probabilities.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "lEQa",
      "code_hash": "f1245c688741b0c0292881cd62069195",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"one-series-per-class\">One series per class</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The graph of a feature has one score per bin and per class. Softmax gives the same probabilities when the same\nnumber is added to the scores of all the classes of a bin. So <code>EBMGraph.from_ebm</code> centres each bin: the mean of\nits scores over the classes is zero. The text has one series of means and bounds for each class, after a\n<code>Class:</code> line.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "PKri",
      "code_hash": "d5519cc8628d56eafa182d773eea71bb",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
